    "case_id": "752",
    "case_summary": "A 29-year-old primigravida woman at 41+2 weeks of pregnancy. No history of gestational diabetes mellitus, preeclampsia, or coagulation disorders, and no anti-coagulation therapy with heparin. Initially admitted for transvaginal delivery, later requiring emergency cesarean section due to amniotic fluid turbidity. Intraoperative blood loss was 400 mL.\n\n15 hours post-procedure: Right lower limb showed full recovery of sensation and muscle strength. Left lower limb showed mild soreness and swelling with manual muscle testing (MMT) grade of 2. At 45.5 hours post-procedure: Hypesthesia below chest level developed, followed by reduction in muscle strength of both lower limbs, progressing to flaccid paralysis.\n\nPre-procedure laboratory results:- Platelet count: 157 × 109/L- Hemoglobin: 132 g/L- Prothrombin time: 10s- APTT: 27s- INR: 0.81- ALT: 31 U/L- AST: 36 U/L- Total bile acid: 9.1 μmol/LImaging:MRI findings:- Space-occupying lesion at left epidural site of T2-3 (12 × 10 × 30 mm)- Axial view showed spinal compression with rightward shift- T1WI and T2FS sequences showed high signals- T2WI displayed mixed high/low signals- No abnormalities at lumbar puncture sitePathological examination:- Confirmed presence of vascular malformation",
    "frontier": [
      "B1",
      "B2",
      "B3"
    ],
